<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/11th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Council of Liubech</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1097">1097</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/11th-century">11th century</a> · Also known as Congress of Liubech, Liubech Congress, Lyubech Congress, Lyubech conference</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Council of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyubech">Liubech</a> was a meeting of the princes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> held in 1097 at Liubech on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dnieper">Dnieper</a>. To end their feuds and unite against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>, the princes, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sviatopolk-ii-of-kiev">Sviatopolk II of Kiev</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir Monomakh</a>, agreed that each should hold his father's patrimony. The agreement was broken almost at once by the blinding of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasylko-rostyslavych">Vasilko of Terebovl</a>, and it confirmed the division of Rus' into hereditary principalities.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/council-of-liubech.jpg" alt="Council of Liubech" width="320" height="464" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: FAJgroupp · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:I_zjazady_ksi%C4%85%C5%BC%C4%85t_Rusi_Kijowskiej._Br%C4%85z,_granit._1997_Lubecz,_Ukraina.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The council met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lyubech">Liubech</a> on 19 October 1097 and is one of the best documented princely meetings of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>. Vladimir Monomakh initiated it to end the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a> war of succession of 1093–1097, in which he and Sviatopolk II had fought Oleg I of Chernigov, a son of Sviatoslav II, over his father's inheritance. Besides Sviatopolk and Oleg, the princes present included <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasylko-rostyslavych">Vasilko Rostislavich</a> and Davyd Sviatoslavich. The meeting aimed to stop the war, to pacify the people and to present a united front against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cumans">Cumans</a>, also known as the Polovtsians.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The council gave each prince his principality as a patrimonial domain, to be inherited by his immediate family. This broke with the rota system, lestvichnoe pravo, followed in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a> for two centuries, under which the throne passed by seniority to the eldest of the dynasty and princes moved gradually upwards from one region to another.</p><p>Sviatopolk II received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/turov">Turov</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pinsk">Pinsk</a> with the title of grand prince. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir Monomakh</a> received Pereyaslavl, the Rostov-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a> lands, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smolensk">Smolensk</a> and Beloozero, and his son Mstislav held Great Novgorod. The sons of Sviatoslav II, Oleg, Davyd and Yaroslav, received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a>, Tmutarakan, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murom">Murom</a>. David Igorevich of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> received Vladimir-in-Volhynia, and Volodar and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasylko-rostyslavych">Vasilko Rostislavich</a> received <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/przemysl">Peremyshl</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/terebovlia">Terebovl</a> and Cherven.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Some scholars hold that the settlement established a feudal system in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus'</a>, while others argue that it created a federative structure. It ended the struggle for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chernigov">Chernigov</a>, but the feuding between the princes continued, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vladimir-ii-monomakh">Vladimir Monomakh</a> called the Council of Uvetichi on 10 August 1100. After Sviatopolk's death in 1113 the citizens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kyiv">Kiev</a> revolted and summoned Vladimir Monomakh to the throne.</p><p>The new arrangement allowed regional centres to grow in power, most notably <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/volhynia">Volhynia</a> and Vladimir-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a>. The period also saw continued work on the legal code known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russkaya-pravda">Russkaya Pravda</a> and the first chronicle compilation, the Initial Compilation or Nachalnyy svod, on which the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primary-chronicle">Primary Chronicle</a> was later based. A monument to the congress of princes by Gennady Ershov was raised in 1997.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Council of Liubech” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Council of Liubech</b> in 1097 tried to end the feuds by declaring that each prince should hold his father's patrimony, which in practice confirmed the division of the land into hereditary principalities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kievan-rus">Kievan Rus' · Governance &amp; Law</a></li><li><blockquote>Under the will of Yaroslav the Wise it passed in 1054 to his son Sviatoslav II, and the <b>Lyubech conference</b> of 1097 confirmed it to Sviatoslav's sons, founding the Olgovichi dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-chernigov">Principality of Chernigov</a></li><li><blockquote>At the <b>Lyubech conference</b> of 1097 it was assigned to the sons of Sviatoslav II of Chernigov, and it became the seat of a junior branch of the Olgovichi, second only to Chernigov itself in their lands.</blockquote><a href="/state/principality-of-novgorod-seversky">Principality of Novgorod-Seversky</a></li></ul></section>
